---
description: Never commit or push without an explicit instruction, and never ask about it
alwaysApply: true
---

# Git Commit & Push — Explicit Instruction Only

Commit and push only when the user's own message says to. Never ask whether to
commit, push, or queue more changes: the queue decides what happens next.

## Required behaviour

1. Complete all code changes requested.
2. Summarise what files changed and why (one sentence per file is enough).
3. End the turn there. No commit, no push, no question about either.

## Do not ask

Do NOT ask "ready to commit and push?", "anything else first?", or any variant,
in prose or via `AskQuestion`. Questions like these interrupt the queue.

`AskQuestion` is still right for decisions that change the work itself — which
approach to take, which data source to use, how to resolve an ambiguity. It is
never right for asking permission to commit or for asking whether more changes
are coming.

## Never auto-commit

Do NOT commit or push on your own initiative, even when:
- The user says "fix X" and you finish the fix
- A previous message implied pushing was expected
- The task feels complete
- A subagent is being used for git operations

The only trigger is an explicit instruction in the user's message, e.g. "commit
and push", "push local changes", "push to Netlify". When that arrives, run the
typecheck rule first, then commit and push everything outstanding.

## Push stuck after typecheck

After `npx tsc --noEmit` succeeds, if `git push` (or the commit+push sequence)
takes more than **30 seconds** or is clearly hanging / blocked on approvals /
auth / sandbox, **stop retrying**. Hand the exact command(s) to the user so
they can run them locally. Do not keep waiting, polling, or re-requesting
permissions for the same push.
